      "title": "CoCliCo - Extreme Surge Level",
      "description": "Extreme Surge Level represents temporary sea level rises caused by storms, crucial for understanding flood risks. Sources: Delft3D-FLOW storm surge model, Copernicus Climate Data Store. Common usage: Flood hazard mapping, storm surge modeling, coastal resilience planning. More Info about the dataset: User Handbook. Variants in the source dataset - rp: 5.0 (5 years), 10.0 (10 years), 20.0 (20 years), 50.0 (50 years), 100.0 (100 years), 200.0 (200 years), 500.0 (500 years), 1000.0 (1000 years) - Frequency at which an extreme event of total water level (related to storms) is expected to occur on average; scenarios: Historical, RCP45 (RCP 4.5), RCP85 (RCP 8.5) - Climate scenarios based on IPCC's Shared Socioeconomic Pathways (SSPs). High-end (if applicable) refers to P83 of SSP5-8.5, taking into account low-confidence processes. Other scenarios use the P50; lon: -29.1, 45.1 - longitude; lat: 27.1, 71.3 - latitude. [Source: This metadata record was automatically extracted from the STAC catalog coclico (https://www.openearth.nl/coclico-workbench/data_catalog/)]",
